This is how you read a regulation. Every record in the database collects **sources**, *requirements* and [guidance](https://regelverk.org) in one place.

## Structure

A regulation consists of several connected layers:

- Source, such as a law or a standard
- Versions of the source over time
- Provisions and individual requirements
- Guidance on how the requirements are applied

## How to find the right one

1. Search by designation, e.g. `AFS 2023:1`
2. Filter by area via the sub-sites
3. Follow the relations between requirements and sources

### Tips

> Always start at the source. Secondary material helps you interpret, but the wording of the source is what counts. Read EU legislation at [EUR-Lex](https://eur-lex.europa.eu) and Swedish statutes in [svensk författningssamling](https://svenskforfattningssamling.se).

Common abbreviations are collected in the registry under `acronyms.yaml`.

| Field | Meaning |
| ----- | ------- |
| Source | Where the requirement comes from |
| Requirement | What must be fulfilled |

```sh
search "ATEX" --scope regelverk
```

